
Executive Access Governance Finding Report
Organization: Boxelder Payroll Systems, Inc.
Finding: Incomplete Termination Access Revocation
Primary SOC 2 Criterion: CC6.2
Risk Rating: High

Executive Summary
A review of Boxelder Payroll Systems' HR records and logical access information identified terminated employees 
who continue to maintain active system access after their employment ended. The most significant exception 
involves former Senior DevOps Engineer Ethan Marbury, whose employment terminated on March 20, 2026 but whose 
Microsoft Entra account remains enabled and whose GitHub organization account remains active with administrator p
rivileges.
A second exception involves former Customer Operations Manager Lauren Pietsch, whose employment terminated on 
February 27, 2026 but whose Microsoft Entra account remains enabled and whose Helprail agent account remains active.
These exceptions indicate a systemic access-governance deficiency rather than isolated administrative errors.

Condition
Terminated personnel have retained active access to Boxelder systems after their employment ended. Specifically, 
Ethan Marbury retains enabled Entra access and active GitHub organization-administrator privileges, while Lauren 
Pietsch retains enabled Entra access and an active Helprail agent account.

Criterion
POL-009, Onboarding and Offboarding Procedure, establishes that access should be granted and revoked reliably 
when personnel join, change roles, or leave Boxelder.
The condition is mapped to SOC 2 Trust Services Criterion CC6.2, which addresses the registration and authorization 
of internal and external users before system credentials are issued and system access is granted. The continued 
existence of active accounts belonging to terminated personnel demonstrates a weakness in the organization's control 
over the authorized user population and the lifecycle management of system access.

Root Cause
The root cause is an incomplete offboarding control design. POL-009 includes GitHub and Helprail account provisioning 
in its onboarding checklist but does not include corresponding GitHub or Helprail revocation activities in its 
offboarding checklist.
As a result, the procedure does not provide IT and HR with a complete system-by-system mechanism for ensuring that all 
access associated with a departing employee is identified and revoked.

Risk and Consequence
The control deficiency creates a risk that former employees can continue accessing corporate systems, sensitive information, 
source code, administrative functionality, and other protected resources after termination.
The risk is particularly significant for privileged accounts. A former engineer retaining GitHub organization-administrator 
privileges could potentially access or alter repositories and organization resources. Persistent orphaned accounts also 
increase the organization's exposure to unauthorized access, credential misuse, data compromise, inadequate accountability, 
and SOC 2 control exceptions.

Recommended Remediation
Management should revise POL-009 so that every system provisioned during onboarding has a corresponding offboarding control. 
GitHub and Helprail should immediately be incorporated into the termination checklist.
IT should also establish and maintain an authoritative application and access inventory and require account owners to confirm 
revocation for each applicable system when an employee or contractor leaves.
As an immediate corrective action, review all access belonging to Ethan Marbury and Lauren Pietsch and revoke any 
inappropriate access. Boxelder should then perform a retrospective reconciliation of terminated employees against active 
accounts across Entra ID, GitHub, Helprail, payroll, VPN/network services, security groups, physical access, and other 
business applications.
Finally, management should implement periodic access reconciliation between the authoritative HR roster and system account 
inventories. Retain evidence of each completed termination review to demonstrate the control operates effectively.

Management Priority
Immediate. Correcting the individual orphaned accounts addresses the current exposure, but revising the underlying offboarding 
process is necessary to prevent recurrence. Management should treat the policy and workflow deficiency as the primary remediation 
item rather than closing the finding solely after the identified accounts are disabled.

